WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.080
Welcome -
Benny and Ludvig Andersson.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.200
Hi.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.240
Hi.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.160
-Welcome.
-Thank you.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:31.920
You'll be performing here
with your band Atlas later on.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.960
While you were rehearsing,
everybody here said-

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.080
-that you're even more at ease on stage
than your father, actually.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.040
Really?

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.040
I feel pretty good on stage.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.440
-But so do you, don't you?
-Sure.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.480
I do, when I get to play together
with other people. Nothing beats that.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:04.680
Were your ambitions for Ludvig
that he should become a star like you?

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:09.600
No. I wanted him to be
whatever the hell pleased him.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.000
I've thought about this.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.680
When I was active I didn't teach him how
to play the piano.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:22.040
I didn't want him to feel: "Maybe dad
thinks that I should..." So, I didn't.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.720
Which I regret a bit now,
since he's doing music anyways.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.200
-It clearly didn't help anyways.
-No.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.840
As far as I know, you work together-

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.040
-both in the studio and at the office.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.280
Yes, we share the same office. Right.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.520
Three or four years ago, we were driving
around in the countryside.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.120
And then you asked,
in your very characteristic manner:

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:57.760
"This thing, you know...
One doesn't live forever and all that."

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:03.680
"Why don't you pop by at work to see
what we're up to."

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.160
"Sure", I said.
But then we talked it over some more.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.960
We decided that someone should
get to know the business-

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:17.280
-for the sake
of some future management of...

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.960
There's a lot that has to be seen to.
A lot of management.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.000
All the old ABBA-stuff, "Chess",
"Duvemåla", "Mama Mia". A lot of things.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.720
All of it. Settlements, contracts,
400 mails per week, and so forth.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.960
-And you're the boss of it all?
-Well, it's my company.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.040
I'm not there hands-on.
I have people working for me.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:43.560
But someone should look into
what I'm doing in all this, once I quit.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.760
That's why I asked Ludvig.
I think it was a pretty good idea.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.440
But... If there's something
you're famous for, its saying "No".

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.000
-I'm great at doing that.
-Why not just have an answerphone:

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.760
"Hi. This is Benny Andersson.
The answer is "No"."

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.080
That would be really cheap.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.720
I am that answerphone.
That's why this job is so easy at times.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.520
The only thing you 
need to know - just say no.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:14.800
But I've been doing this for a while.
I'm 64.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.720
The eagerness is not as strong
as it used to be.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.560
It's just as fun, but you're not as...

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:27.040
It's nice having a young - when I say:
"There's no point in building a studio."

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:30.040
He says: "Sure, there is."
And then we build one.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:35.880
We've built a fantastic studio
on Skeppsholmen. It's a great project.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:40.240
When you get those ABBA-requests-

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.120
-do you need to ask the other members,
or is it just you who...

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:50.320
If one of us says "no", it's no. "May we
use "Dancing Queen" in our movie?"

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.680
And we say: "No."
We get a lot of requests of that nature.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.800
But sometimes there are interesting
things. A game, whatever it's called.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:05.680
What's the name of that recent one?
"Sing Star" or something.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.320
A karaoke game with our old video clips.
Those things can be fun to do.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:15.720
So, we said "yes". If one of us says
"yes", all the others have to be asked.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:22.120
Is that what happened some years ago
when Madonna used "Gimme Gimme"?

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.800
-Do you know which one I mean?
-Sure.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:31.800
That's right. We normally always say
"no" when it comes to sampling stuff.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:36.400
But she wrote such a nice letter
and sent her personal assistant.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:42.640
I said: "We'll have to listen to it
first." It was great and we said "yes".

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.520
In that case I only need to speak with
Björn, because it's a copyright issue.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.400
But do you get a lot of requests
from sheikhs from Bahrain-

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:56.960
-who try to get you to reunite?

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.680
There haven't been that many sheikhs
from Bahrain.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.920
You only meet those
when you're buying race-horses.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.200
-Horses are your big hobby.
-It's more than a hobby.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.040
A bit more than a hobby.
How many horses do you have?

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.320
I often get that question.
About sixteen horses.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:24.080
-Sixteen horses?
-Foals, brood-mares...

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.920
...and horses in training,
in Sweden and in England.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.680
I've been doing this for 28 years now.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.680
-How did it all start?
-A family situation. Too long a story.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.400
But this family situation
made me do this-

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:43.200
-to maintain good communications
with my stepson's father.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.280
He's married to a race-horse coach.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.000
I figured it'd be a good...

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:58.440
You've spent quite a lot of money in
order to maintain this communication.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.240
-Indeed.
-Over time.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.200
It's an incredibly efficient way
of spending money.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.960
I actually know that, because you said
- when we met earlier...

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.680
You gave us a tip, that we should
bet money on Jive. Last weekend.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:17.640
-Yes. It didn't go too well.
-No, it did not.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.520
I bet everything I had.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.640
Do you get the feeling he's sick of ABBA
and would rather work with horses?

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:31.560
Maybe not with horses, but that
ABBA-thing is a thing of the past.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:35.160
That's the feeling I get.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:40.320
But the horses aren't that important.
I haven't replaced one with the other.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:46.120
Ludvig's right. There isn't much life
in ABBA - since 30 years now.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.920
Is he a kind dad?

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.440
You do look very kind.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:58.160
-Do I look kind?
-Yes. I grew up with ABBA.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.640
And I always thought you looked
like the kindest one of the lot.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:06.520
-He's definitely the kindest one.
-Are you an ambitious person?

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:13.880
Yes, I believe I am. It's just a bit
difficult to know what to aim at.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:18.280
I'm doing great. I have my orchestra -
the Benny Andersson Orchestra.

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:23.640
It began as a hobby project I'd work on
when I wasn't doing bigger things.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.680
I wrote one or two songs
and we made a record.

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:32.800
But now it has become
my main occupation.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:38.200
I feel very good about this.
It's nice not having to keep pushing.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.080
It's an age-thing, that's for sure.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.560
But that's when I come in
and I say:

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.720
"Should you really focus so much
on that orchestra?"

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:51.640
"Don't you want to do something else
that's equally fun?"

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.360
"Make another movie", he says.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:08:01.240
When we read about you,
a picture of you as an idealist emerges.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:06.760
For instance, you pay your taxes
with joy.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:14.280
You've donated a large sum
to Feminist Initiative.

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:19.960
And when you do that, is it because
you're a political person?

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:25.000
Isn't everybody? I am interested
in how things work, naturally.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.680
I know what I think and so forth,
but I'm not a political person as such.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:36.560
I'm not particularly aggressive, but
I am interested in society. Definitely.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:38.880
-Are you a feminist?
-Yes.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.600
Do you feel like you missed out
on the 70's?

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:47.520
That you would've been perfect
in a protest band in the 70's?

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.520
-ABBA wasn't a protest band.
-No, it wasn't.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.440
And we did have a certain tunnel vision.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:01.160
We'd be in the studio and only focus
on what we were doing.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.840
We wrote as many songs as possible that
were good enough to record.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.200
And that's where we were
and remained.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:15.640
I regret that a bit. So much happened
in 1968, 1970, 1972, those years-

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:20.920
-that really would've suited me, but it
wasn't the right moment for that.

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:23.280
I'm making up for it now.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.880
You never chose a....
You never chose a different lifestyle.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:32.760
You could have moved to a tax haven,
lived in London...

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:39.720
And lived a different life after ABBA,
more jet-set like.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:42.240
-You never chose that life.
-No.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.760
-Why not?
-If I imagined living abroad...

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:52.000
The only place I could imagine myself
moving to is Norway. Seriously.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:10:00.560
We're neighbours, we have...
We may not share the same culture...

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:06.160
Moving to a place where nobody knows
where you come from, who Tumba is-

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.200
-or Lennart Hyland... Right?
It's hard.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:13.840
You can get acquainted
with their lifestyle-

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.360
-but an Englishman couldn't be bothered
with our forests.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:21.400
Or, for that matter... If...

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:26.880
Let's say I start talking about
Thoralf Engan. They'd just go: "Huh?"

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:30.120
Maybe it wasn't such a good example.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:34.960
-Oddvar Brå.
-Oddvar Brå. Yeah, now we're talking.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:39.040
So, whenever you mention this,
you know that you'll have...

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:42.600
There are 250 people here
who know what I'm talking about.

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:46.760
You can't do without that.
That's why the question never arose.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.920
-You're born after ABBA.
-Yes. It was my fault.

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:54.480
What does ABBA mean to you?

00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:00.320
ABBA is something very nice, of course.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:05.480
I personally find the fact
that they never reunite to be quite sad-

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:09.240
-even if I understand why,
but it would've been fun.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:15.600
It's there somewhere, but it's difficult
to say. I have nothing to compare with.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:20.480
You've been asked this 1 000 times
and it's clear that you don't want to.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:25.000
-But have you ever talked about it?
-Sorry?

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:28.560
-Have you ever talked about a reunion?
-No.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:32.520
-Did you ever sit down all of you to...
-No.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:36.160
We got an offer about ten years ago,
which I didn't believe in.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:41.560
We were offered a huge amount of money
for 100 concerts over a year-

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:46.960
-sponsored by whatever. It was such
a sum that we called each other: "So?"

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:49.560
And we all answered: "No way."

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:54.240
-So, it's rather easy.
-Would one of you like to?

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:57.400
-No, nobody wants to.
-You're all agreed.

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:02.080
-For now.
-It's never going to happen, then.

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:06.360
-"For now"... Great. We'll see.
-We'll see.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:09.080
-Are you pushing him a bit?
-I am, yes.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:12.920
Welcome, Björn Ulvaeus.

